  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1123 AM MDT Sat May 9 2026\n\nVery little change from previous forecast packages with generally \ndry weather conditions and above average temperatures through the \nperiod.\n\n...SATURDAY & SUNDAY...\n\nUnpretentious northwest flow aloft will continue today as the \neastern periphery of an upper level ridge continues to nose in \nfrom the west across the region. Modest mid-level moisture within \nthe northwest will will promote cumulus growth during the \nafternoon hours across Grant/Sierra/Otero Counties. High \nresolution guidance and associated forecast soundings show a \nslight chances of rain showers across those areas. However, virga \nshowers will be most common with any development as the low levels\nremain dry, with maybe a few sprinkles reaching the surface. \nGeneral afternoon winds will be light out of the west, with \nisolated gusts to 30 mph within the vicinity of virga shower \ndevelopment. High temperatures this afternoon will warm into the \nlow to middle 90s across the desert lowlands, 5-10 degrees above \nthe seasonal average.\n\nBy tonight and into Sunday morning, a backdoor/sidedoor front will \nbring breezy winds out of the north/east (dependent on location) for \nareas along and east of the the RGV. A weak disturbance passing off \nto the east will bring weak but somewhat sufficient forcing will \npromote a slight (10-30%) chance of showers and thunderstorms over \nthe Sacramento Mtns Sunday afternoon, with virga showers elsewhere \nacross the CWA. The primary threat with any development will be \ngusty winds, with high res ensemble guidance showing the potential \nfor gusts up to 40 mph. High temperatures Sunday will be hovering in \nthe low 90s across the lowlands. The aforementioned backdoor front \nwill eventually push further west Sunday night and into Monday \nmorning. Winds will become gusty along the west of slopes of area \nmountain chains and desert lowland sky islands.\n\n...MONDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY...\n\nBy the start of next week, upper level ridge of high pressure will \nslowly drift east across NM. In response, surface pressure gradient \nwill relax and lead to light afternoon breezes each day through the \nwork week. As 500mb heights rise, temperatures will also respond,\nwarming into the middle 90s, with middle to upper 90s Tuesday \nthrough Thursday. Upper level Pacific storm system coming onshore \nover NorCal on Thursday will break down the upper high as it \nslowly translates east. Modest moisture will lift north out of \nMexico ahead of the aforementioned system. As of now, rain/storm \nchances don't look impressive at all, but the mention of slight \nchances of rain/storms could be added to the grids in future \nforecast packages.\n\n...THURSDAY & FRIDAY...\n\nAforementioned storm system will move west to east well north of \nthe forecast area with breezy winds returning during that \ntimeframe. Temperatures will remain in the 90s across the desert \nlowlands Thursday and Friday.",
